**`@WebMvcTest` — controller in isolation**

```java
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest {

    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
    @MockBean OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void returns404WhenOrderMissing() throws Exception {
        when(orderService.find("abc")).thenReturn(Optional.empty());

        mvc.perform(get("/orders/abc"))
            .andExpect(status().isNotFound());
    }
}
```

**`@DataJpaTest` — repository in isolation**

```java
@DataJpaTest
class OrderRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired OrderRepository repo;

    @Test
    void findsByCustomerWithStatus() {
        repo.save(new Order("c1", Status.PAID));
        repo.save(new Order("c1", Status.SHIPPED));

        var paid = repo.findByCustomerIdAndStatus("c1", Status.PAID);
        assertThat(paid).hasSize(1);
    }
}
```

**Operational notes**
- `@WebMvcTest` does not scan `@Service` or `@Repository` beans — mock them with `@MockBean`
- `@DataJpaTest` defaults to H2; tests are wrapped in a transaction that rolls back after each test
- Use `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = NONE)` with Testcontainers for production-fidelity DB tests
- `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)` starts a real server on a random port for end-to-end HTTP tests

**The cache key**
- Configuration classes (`@SpringBootTest`, `@ContextConfiguration`)
- Active profiles (`@ActiveProfiles`)
- Property sources (`@TestPropertySource`)
- Mock bean definitions (`@MockBean`, `@SpyBean`)
- Web environment (MOCK, RANDOM_PORT, etc.)

**`@MockBean` is the silent cache killer**
- Each unique combination of `@MockBean` declarations produces a different cache key
- `TestA` mocks `UserService`; `TestB` mocks `UserService` + `PaymentService` → two contexts
- In a 100-test suite this can mean 20+ context creations instead of 2-3

**Stabilize mocks across tests**

```java
abstract class BaseControllerTest {
    @MockBean protected UserService userService;
    @MockBean protected PaymentService paymentService;
}

@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest extends BaseControllerTest { ... }

@WebMvcTest(InvoiceController.class)
class InvoiceControllerTest extends BaseControllerTest { ... }
```

**Monitor cache behavior**
- Set `logging.level.org.springframework.test.context.cache=DEBUG`
- Logs show every cache hit and miss with the cache key
- Goal: under 5 distinct contexts for an entire test suite
- More than that means mock or property strategy needs cleanup

**`@DirtiesContext` and other forced evictions**
- `@DirtiesContext` forces context disposal — only use when state mutation is unavoidable (e.g., embedded broker shared across tests)
- Maximum cache size is bounded; old contexts are evicted by LRU policy

**Shared @TestConfiguration with reusable static containers**

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class IntegrationTestContainers {

    @Bean
    @ServiceConnection
    PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres() {
        return new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine")
            .withReuse(true);
    }

    @Bean
    @ServiceConnection
    KafkaContainer kafka() {
        return new KafkaContainer(DockerImageName.parse("confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.5.0"))
            .withReuse(true);
    }

    @Bean
    @ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
    GenericContainer<?> redis() {
        return new GenericContainer<>("redis:7-alpine")
            .withExposedPorts(6379)
            .withReuse(true);
    }
}
```

**Use it in any integration test**

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(IntegrationTestContainers.class)
class OrderFlowIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired OrderService orders;
    @Autowired KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafka;

    @Test
    void persistsAndPublishes() {
        var order = orders.place(new NewOrder("c1", List.of("sku-1")));

        assertThat(order.getId()).isNotNull();
        kafka.send("orders", order.getId().toString());
    }
}
```

**What `@ServiceConnection` gives you**
- Spring Boot detects the container type and auto-configures the connection (JDBC URL, Kafka bootstrap, Redis host/port)
- No `@DynamicPropertySource`, no manual `spring.datasource.url` wiring
- The `@TestConfiguration` is shared across every `@SpringBootTest` that imports it — same context cache, same container

**For Spring Boot < 3.1 or special cases**

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class LegacyIntegrationTest {

    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine")
        .withReuse(true);

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void registerProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }
}
```

**Why static fields matter**
- `static` ensures the container starts once for the JVM, not once per test class
- `@Container` lifecycle annotation is provided by `@Testcontainers`
- Reuse + static = the container is shared even across multiple test classes

**Pitfall — instance field containers**
- Non-static `@Container` fields start a new container per test class
- That breaks Spring's context cache because container URLs differ per class
- Always make integration containers `static final`
